<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Guipuzcoan Company of Caracas</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1728">1728</a>–<a href="/years/1785">1785</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Guipuzcoan Company of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caracas">Caracas</a> was a Basque trading company chartered by the Spanish Crown in 1728 with a monopoly on trade between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and the province of Venezuela. It shipped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cacao">cacao</a> to Spain, fought smuggling and built ports and forts, but its low prices for cacao angered planters and provoked the revolt of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-francisco-de-leon">Juan Francisco de León</a> in 1749. It lost its monopoly and was merged into the Royal Company of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a> in 1785.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/guipuzcoan-company-of-caracas.jpg" alt="Guipuzcoan Company of Caracas" width="165" height="144" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Compania Guipuzcoana Madrid · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Guipuzcoan_Company_Caracas.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Since 1503 all Spanish trade with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a> had passed through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a>, and after 1717 Cádiz, under a monopoly that shut out other ports such as San Sebastián. In Venezuela the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/criollo-people">Creole</a> landholders preferred to trade with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a>, British and French merchants, and between 1700 and 1728 only five ships sailed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> to the province.</p><p>The company grew out of negotiations between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-v-of-spain">Philip V of Spain</a> and the Basque governments after his campaign in the western Basque districts, on a plan put forward by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gipuzkoa">Gipuzkoa</a>. Founded by wealthy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basques">Basques</a> of that province and based at first in San Sebastián, it received its royal decree on 25 September 1728, as part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bourbon-reforms">Bourbon Reforms</a>, with the aim of breaking the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> hold on the cocoa trade.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Its first four ships left San Sebastián in 1730 with a crew of 561, and met open hostility from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/criollo-people">Creoles</a>, who refused to sell cocoa and rose against the newcomers and the Spanish garrison. The company kept its own warships and privateers, overloaded its ships with cocoa and controlled the ports of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-guaira">La Guaira</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puerto-cabello">Puerto Cabello</a>. It encouraged tobacco, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indigo">indigo</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a>, and its agent José de Iturriaga y Aguirre led a border expedition in 1750–1761 that founded settlements in Guayana. In 1751 the Crown moved its headquarters to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a>, against the wishes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gipuzkoa">Gipuzkoa</a>.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-navy">Royal Navy</a> captured seven warships and fifteen merchantmen of the company on 8 January 1780 and two more merchant ships on 15 January 1782. With trade with Venezuela opened after 1778, the Crown ended the charter in 1784.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>In the Basque Country the company kept forges working and supplied the arms factories of Soraluze and Tolosa; another outcome was the founding in Bergara in 1765 of the Royal Basque Society of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quakers">Friends</a> of the Country, a model for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/age-of-enlightenment">Enlightenment</a> societies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. In Venezuela it secured the place of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caracas">Caracas</a> within the captaincy-general: the Real Audiencia was sited there in 1786, and a merchants' <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guild</a> replaced the company in 1793.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Guipuzcoan Company of Caracas” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>From 1728 the <b>Guipuzcoan Company of Caracas</b> held a trade monopoly, provoking the revolt of Juan Francisco de León in 1749.</blockquote><a href="/state/province-of-venezuela">Province of Venezuela</a></li></ul></section>
